{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":457396,"items":[{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,5]],"date-time":"2026-05-05T13:35:22Z","timestamp":1777988122689,"version":"3.51.4"},"reference-count":0,"publisher":"Instituto Chico Mendes de Conservacao da Biodiversidade - ICMBio","issue":"1","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2019,5,15]],"date-time":"2019-05-15T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1557878400000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by-nc-nd\/4.0"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["Biodivers. Bras."],"abstract":"<jats:p>Tropical Peat swamp forests are one of tropical wetland ecosystems that has dynamic link between land and water and also transition zone where the water flow, the nutrients cycling and the solar energy combine together to create an irreplaceable environment of hydrology, soils and vegetation. The peat swamp forest functioned as carbon sink and carbon storage, their presence have been beneficial to human kind. During dry period, tropical peat swamps are prone to forest fires and causes transboundary haze in South East Asia in 2005 and 2015. Local community around tropical peat swamp can play an important role in integrated peat forest fire management but little research has been done to understand their knowledge and attitude towards forest fire. A study was conducted to assess the attitudes and perception of villagers affected by forest fire. Likert questionaire is used to elicit repondents attitudes and perception by asking the degree to which the individual agree or disagree with the statement. Respondents from all three groups agreed that forest fire would cause air pollution, soil erosion, green house effect and thick haze, so indirectly cause disease like asthma, respiratory disease, skin infection. Villager affected by forest fire supported immediate fire suppression and agree that forest fire is a big problem in this country. The findings from this study will help the relevant authorities to formulate a better integrated forest fire management programme.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.37002\/biodiversidadebrasileira.v9i1.1210","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,9]],"date-time":"2025-10-09T21:41:02Z","timestamp":1760046062000},"page":"44","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Integrated forest fire management in tropical peat swamp forest:"],"prefix":"10.37002","volume":"9","author":[{"given":"Ahmad","family":"Ainuddin Nuruddin","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Maichal","family":"Isthyben Sawairnathan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"23146","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2019,5,15]]},"container-title":["Biodiversidade Brasileira"],"link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/revistaeletronica.icmbio.gov.br\/index.php\/BioBR\/article\/download\/1210\/682","content-type":"application\/pdf","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"text-mining"},{"URL":"https:\/\/revistaeletronica.icmbio.gov.br\/index.php\/BioBR\/article\/download\/1210\/682","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,9]],"date-time":"2025-10-09T21:41:03Z","timestamp":1760046063000},"score":34.326954,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/revistaeletronica.icmbio.gov.br\/index.php\/BioBR\/article\/view\/1210"}},"subtitle":["role of local community"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2019,5,15]]},"references-count":0,"journal-issue":{"issue":"1","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2019,5,28]]}},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.37002\/biodiversidadebrasileira.v9i1.1210","ISSN":["2236-2886"],"issn-type":[{"value":"2236-2886","type":"electronic"}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2019,5,15]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2022,4,5]],"date-time":"2022-04-05T09:30:44Z","timestamp":1649151044186},"reference-count":0,"publisher":"Experiment","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"DOI":"10.18258\/24536","type":"report","created":{"date-parts":[[2022,3,20]],"date-time":"2022-03-20T08:00:21Z","timestamp":1647763221000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["How are rural communities on the edge of the largest tropical peat swamp forest in the Congo Basin using peat resources?"],"prefix":"10.18258","author":[{"given":"Shona Jenkins","family":"Shona Jenkins","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"7579","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2022,2,9]]},"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2022,3,20]],"date-time":"2022-03-20T08:00:22Z","timestamp":1647763222000},"score":34.2032,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/experiment.com\/projects\/how-are-rural-communities-on-the-edge-of-the-largest-tropical-peat-swamp-forest-in-the-congo-basin-using-peat-resources"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022,2,9]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.18258\/24536","published":{"date-parts":[[2022,2,9]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,23]],"date-time":"2026-07-23T02:02:52Z","timestamp":1784772172714,"version":"3.55.0"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"group-title":"SSRN","reference-count":74,"publisher":"Elsevier BV","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>The hydrological condition of peatlands influences their capacity to function as carbon sinks and to recover following disturbance and degradation. In efforts to reduce peat fires and associated carbon emissions, peatland hydrological restoration has been a key priority for the Indonesian government. Yet relationships between the peat hydrological properties that underpin fire risk and restoration success are not well understood. Here, we compared water table depth (WTD), soil water content (SWC), soil temperature, soil matric potential (SMP) and soil electrical conductivity (EC) between intact and degraded (historically drained and burnt by wildfire) peatland areas in Central Kalimantan, Indonesia, from August 2024 to November 2025 (16 months). At the degraded area, WTD was 1.5-fold more temporally variable than at the intact area, although WTD at both areas fell below the predetermined -40 cm fire risk threshold in the dry season of 2025, despite the prevailing La Ni\u00f1a\/neutral conditions. Differences in wet season WTD and SWC are likely indicative of changes in peat physical properties at the degraded area, following several decades of drainage. Soil temperatures, surface EC, and matric potential were higher at the degraded area than at the intact area, with implications for forest regeneration and carbon cycling. We discuss the implications of our findings in the context of peat fire prevention and hydrological restoration in drained tropical peatlands.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7163970","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,23]],"date-time":"2026-07-23T01:39:16Z","timestamp":1784770756000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Soil hydrology in a tropical peat swamp forest: Impacts of drainage and degradation"],"prefix":"10.2139","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8905-9919","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Sarah","family":"Treby","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8836-4815","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Samantha","family":"Grover","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sopa  Nindia Ati","family":"Ningsih","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-9992-3003","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Hannah  AR","family":"Kennedy","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Andri","family":"Thomas","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9807-4360","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Laura  L. B.","family":"Graham","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"78","reference":[{"key":"ref1","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","DOI":"10.3402\/tellusb.v65i0.20792","article-title":"Sustained large stimulation of soil heterotrophic respiration rate and its temperature sensitivity by soil warming in a cool-temperate forested peatland","volume":"65","author":"M Aguilos","year":"2013","journal-title":"Tellus B: Chemical and Physical Meteorology"},{"key":"ref2","author":"A Asadi","year":"2009"},{"issue":"1 7","key":"ref3","first-page":"63","article-title":"Greenhouse gas emissions intensity of global croplands","volume":"7","author":"K M Carlson","year":"2016","journal-title":"Nature Climate Change"},{"key":"ref4","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"9351","DOI":"10.1029\/2019WR025411","article-title":"Scalar Simulation and Parameterization of Water Table Dynamics in Tropical Peatlands","volume":"55","author":"A R Cobb","year":"2019","journal-title":"Water Resour. Res"},{"key":"ref5","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"1","DOI":"10.18637\/jss.v034.i02","article-title":"Beta regression in R","volume":"34","author":"F Cribari-Neto","year":"2010","journal-title":"J. Stat. Softw"},{"key":"ref6","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"76","DOI":"10.47352\/jmans.2774-3047.189","article-title":"Influence of Water Table and Peat Thickness on Dissolved Organic Carbon of Tropical Peat Soil with Sulfidic Substratum from Central Kalimantan, Indonesia","volume":"4","author":"Z Damanik","year":"2024","journal-title":"Journal of Multidisciplinary Applied Natural Science"},{"key":"ref7","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","DOI":"10.3389\/fgeoc.2025.14