l-title":"Agric. For. Meteorol"},{"key":"ref61","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"3227","DOI":"10.1002\/hyp.10436","article-title":"Seasonal variation in albedo and radiation exchange between a burned and unburned forested peatland: Implications for peatland evaporation","volume":"29","author":"D K Thompson","year":"2015","journal-title":"Hydrol. Process"},{"key":"ref62","article-title":"Principles for equitable and resilient tropical peatland restoration in Central Kalimantan","volume":"32","author":"T D Toumbourou","year":"2024","journal-title":"Indonesia. Restor. Ecol"},{"key":"ref63","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"186","DOI":"10.1016\/j.ecohyd.2022.12.007","article-title":"Impacts of feral grazers and unseasonal summer flooding on floodplain carbon dynamics: A case study","volume":"23","author":"S Treby","year":"2023","journal-title":"Ecohydrology & Hydrobiology"},{"key":"ref64","article-title":"Soil hydrology in a drained tropical peatland in Central Kalimantan, Indonesia: Implications for land management","author":"S Treby","year":"2026","journal-title":"Pedosphere"},{"key":"ref65","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","DOI":"10.1002\/hyp.70240","article-title":"Hydrological Fluxes From a Drained Tropical Peatland: Evapotranspiration Consistent Despite Large Fluctuations in Precipitation and Soil Moisture","volume":"39","author":"S Treby","year":"2025","journal-title":"Hydrol. Process"},{"key":"ref66","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","DOI":"10.1016\/j.geodrs.2024.e00896","article-title":"Exploring the physical properties of Australian alpine soils to inform ecosystem restoration","volume":"39","author":"S Treby","year":"2024","journal-title":"Geoderma Regional"},{"key":"ref67","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"11","DOI":"10.1038\/ngeo2325","article-title":"Global vulnerability of peatlands to fire and carbon loss","volume":"8","author":"M R Turetsky","year":"2015","journal-title":"Nat. Geosci"},{"key":"ref68","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"159","DOI":"10.1016\/S0921-8009(03)00139-3","article-title":"The economics of slash and burn: a case study of the 1997-1998 Indonesian forest fires","volume":"46","author":"A Varma","year":"2003","journal-title":"Ecological Economics"},{"key":"ref69","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"135","DOI":"10.1201\/b10708-6","author":"E Verry","year":"2011","journal-title":"Physical Properties of Organic Soils. Peatland Biogeochemistry and Watershed Hydrology at the Marcell Experimental Forest"},{"key":"ref70","author":"E Walle","year":"2021","journal-title":"A hydrological comparison of drained, pristine, and recently rewetted bogs. Early signs of improvement?"},{"key":"ref71","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"639","DOI":"10.1111\/ejss.12251","article-title":"Multi-scale analysis of electrical conductivity of peatlands for the assessment of peat properties","volume":"66","author":"J Walter","year":"2015","journal-title":"Eur. J. Soil Sci"},{"key":"ref72","author":"H Wickham","year":"2014","journal-title":"Grammar of Data Manipulation. CRAN: Contributed Packages"},{"key":"ref73","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","first-page":"212","DOI":"10.1016\/j.catena.2007.07.010","article-title":"Peat-water interrelationships in a tropical peatland ecosystem in Southeast Asia","volume":"73","author":"J H M W\ufffdsten","year":"2008","journal-title":"Catena (Amst)"},{"key":"ref74","first-page":"25","article-title":"Peat subsidence and its practical implications: a case study in Malaysia","volume":"78","author":"J H M W\ufffdsten","year":"1997","journal-title":"Biogeosciences"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,23]],"date-time":"2026-07-23T01:43:25Z","timestamp":1784771005000},"score":33.375713,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.ssrn.com\/abstract=7163970"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"references-count":74,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7163970","published":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"subtype":"preprint"},{"institution":[{"name":"Chulalongkorn University","acronym":["CU"],"place":["Bangkok, Thailand"],"department":["Petroleum Geoscience"]}],"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,27]],"date-time":"2025-03-27T04:11:29Z","timestamp":1743048689179,"version":"3.40.3"},"reference-count":0,"publisher":"Office of Academic Resources, Chulalongkorn University","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>Peatlands, as an important carbon sink, store a large amount of atmospheric carbon. However, peatlands are vulnerable to environmental alteration due to anthropogenic and natural processes. While peatlands are degraded, carbon dioxide emissions arise. The Kuan Kreng peat swamp forest is the second largest peatland in Thailand and is in coastal wetland serving for carbon stock in the subsurface. To assess carbon stock in peatland, the thickness and distribution of the peat layer are necessary. This study determines physical and chemical characteristics of the peatlands in the coastal wetland, southern Thailand by conventional core study and geophysical surveys, including ground penetrating radar (GPR) and electrical resistivity imaging (ERI). There is a significant relationship between chemical and physical properties from the peat layer, which is useful to approximately predict the physical properties and peat layers in the geophysical profiles. The resistivity profiles exhibit a high resistivity response, between 10.99 and 158.94 ohm-m, with an average of 52.52\u00b115.10 ohm-m, interpreted as the peat layers in the shallow subsurface. The high resolution GPR profiles provide an average GPR velocity in peat of 0.040 m\/ns and a relative dielectric constant of 54.9 for the peat layers. The peat layer is characterized by a high amplitude of reflection in the GPR profiles. The thickness of the peat layers was estimated from the geophysical surveys and the drilling cores having an average thickness of 0.22\u00b10.04 cm. Average values of bulk density (0.19\u00b10.03 g\/cm\u00ad3) and TOC (31.18\u00b14.13 wt. %) from the drilling core samples are used to calculate the carbon density in the peat layers giving a result of 59.24\u00b117.20 Kg C\/m3. Hence, the carbon stock in study area two is estimated to be at least 823.02\u00b1238.96 tons carbon, which accounts for 3,020.49\u00b1877.00 tons carbon dioxide. Therefore, considering the entire area of the Kuan Kreng peat swamp forest with the assumption of consistent thickness and distribution of the peat layer, the carbon stock is estimated at a minimum of 9.69\u00b10.35 Mt.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.58837\/chula.the.2023.939","type":"dissertation","created":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,26]],"date-time":"2025-03-26T10:27:36Z","timestamp":1742984856000},"approved":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Geophysical characteristics of kuan kreng peat swamp forest at thale noi area"],"prefix":"10.58837","author":[{"sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Theethach","family":"Phiranram","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"37481","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,26]],"date-time":"2025-03-26T10:27:37Z","timestamp":1742984857000},"score":33.202324,"degree":["Master of Science"],"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/digiverse.chula.ac.th\/Info\/item\/dc:96289"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[null]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.58837\/chula.the.2023.939"},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2022,3,31]],"date-time":"2022-03-31T21:18:23Z","timestamp":1648761503180},"reference-count":0,"publisher":"Forest Research Institute Malaysia","issue":"1","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["JTFS"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2022,2,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The mechanism proposed to explain tree monodominance in tropical forests is that the dominant species forms a dense canopy and produces shade-tolerant seedlings, which together favor selfreplacement. Under this hypothesis, seedlings of monodominant species should have limited ability to respond to drastic increases in understory light, like those resulting from logging. Therefore, monodominant species should lose their seedling dominance after logging-induced canopy opening. To test this hypothesis, the current study measured seedling survival and growth of the monodominant species, Prioria copaifera, and its main competitor, Pentaclethra macroloba, in two forest stands that differ in logging history and in an unlogged stand in southeastern Costa Rica. Although, growth rates and survival of previously established seedlings were similar for both species across the three stands, seedling survival and growth decreased as light increased, with the effect being more pronounced for Prioria than for Pentaclethra. The study also investigated the ability of Prioria to respond to changing light environments by transplanting seedlings into logging-induced canopy gaps. Contrary to prediction, Prioria seedlings survived and grew better in gap centers than in gap edges or under a closed canopy. This result contrasts with established seedlings that cannot acclimate to changes in light conditions. Therefore, we conclude that continued Prioria dominance in selectively logged forests depends more on seedling production after canopy disturbance than on the established seedling present before disturbances.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.26525\/jtfs2022.34.1.34","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2022,1,24]],"date-time":"2022-01-24T08:20:09Z","timestamp":1643012409000},"page":"34-47","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["EFFECTS OF CANOPY OPENNESS ON SEEDLING SURVIVAL AND GROWTH AFTER SELECTIVE LOGGING IN A MONODOMINANT LOWLAND SWAMP FOREST IN COSTA RICA"],"prefix":"10.26525","volume":"34","author":[{"given":"OJ","family":"Valverde-Barrantes","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"JA","family":"Hogan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"OJ","family":"Rocha","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"11054","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2022,1,26]]},"container-title":["JOURNAL OF TROPICAL FOREST SCIENCE"],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2022,1,24]],"date-time":"2022-01-24T08:20:13Z","timestamp":1643012413000},"score":33.144444,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/info.frim.gov.my\/infocenter_applications\/jtfsonline\/jtfs\/v34n1\/34-47.pdf"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022,1,26]]},"references-count":0,"journal-issue":{"issue":"1","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2022,1,26]]},"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2022,2,1]]}},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.26525\/jtfs2022.34.1.34","ISSN":["0128-1283","2521-9847"],"issn-type":[{"value":"0128-1283","type":"print"},{"value":"2521-9847","type":"electronic"}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2022,1,26]]}}],"items-per-page":5,"query":{"start-index":0,"search-terms":null}}}